Line 1: $(1+t,\,2,\,t)$. Line 2: $(s,\,2,\,2-s)$.
Equating coordinates:
\begin{align*}
1+t&=s,\tag{1}\\
2&=2,\tag{2}\\
t&=2-s.\tag{3}
\end{align*}
From (1), $s=1+t$. Substituting into (3): $t=2-(1+t)=1-t$, so $2t=1$ and $t=\tfrac12$; then $s=1+\tfrac12=\tfrac32$. Check (1): $1+\tfrac12=\tfrac32=s$.
The intersection point is $(1+\tfrac12,\,2,\,\tfrac12)=\left(\tfrac32,\,2,\,\tfrac12\right)$.

